Code":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2022.101733"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8958531"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8958531?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":14,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["pdb"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2022-04-26","dateOfCreation":"2022-02-17","dateOfRevision":"2023-07-12","electronicPublicationDate":"2022-02-15","firstPublicationDate":"2022-02-15","firstIndexDate":"2022-02-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2022-04-11","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"35505245","source":"MED","pmid":"35505245","pmcid":"PMC9089323","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC9089323"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41564-022-01109-9","title":"Clostridium sporogenes uses reductive Stickland metabolism in the gut to generate ATP and produce circulating metabolites.","authorString":"Liu Y, Chen H, Van Treuren W, Hou BH, Higginbottom SK, Dodd D.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu Y","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Yuanyuan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pathology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen H","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Haoqing","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4290-6092"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pathology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Van Treuren W","lastName":"Van Treuren","firstName":"William","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hou BH","lastName":"Hou","firstName":"Bi-Huei","initials":"BH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pathology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Higginbottom SK","lastName":"Higginbottom","firstName":"Steven K","initials":"SK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4609-6840"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Dodd D","lastName":"Dodd","firstName":"Dylan","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6210-6239"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pathology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA. ddodd2@stanford.edu."},{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA. ddodd2@stanford.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6210-6239"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4290-6092"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4609-6840"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"7","journalIssueId":3352650,"dateOfPublication":"2022 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2022,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nat Microbiol","title":"Nature microbiology","essn":"2058-5276","issn":"2058-5276","isoabbreviation":"Nat Microbiol","nlmid":"101674869"},"printPublicationDate":"2022-05-01"},"pubYear":"2022","pageInfo":"695-706","abstractText":"Gut bacteria face a key problem in how they capture enough energy to sustain their growth and physiology. The gut bacterium Clostridium sporogenes obtains its energy by utilizing amino acids in pairs, coupling the oxidation of one to the reduction of another-the Stickland reaction. Oxidative pathways produce ATP via substrate-level phosphorylation, whereas reductive pathways are thought to balance redox. In the present study, we investigated whether these reductive pathways are also linked to energy generation and the production of microbial metabolites that may circulate and impact host physiology. Using metabolomics, we find that, during growth in vitro, C. sporogenes produces 15 metabolites, 13 of which are present in the gut of C. sporogenes-colonized mice. Four of these compounds are reductive Stickland metabolites that circulate in the blood of gnotobiotic mice and are also detected in plasma from healthy humans. Gene clusters for reductive Stickland pathways suggest involvement of electron transfer proteins, and experiments in vitro demonstrate that reductive metabolism is coupled to ATP formation and not just redox balance. Genetic analysis points to the broadly conserved Rnf complex as a key coupling site for energy transduction. Rnf complex mutants show aberrant amino acid metabolism in a defined medium and are attenuated for growth in the mouse gut, demonstrating a role of the Rnf complex in Stickland metabolism and gut colonization. Our findings reveal that the production of circulating metabolites by a commensal bacterium within the host gut is linked to an ATP-yielding redox process.","affiliation":"Department of Pathology, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NCCIH NIH HHS","acronym":"AT","grantId":"R01 AT011396","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","grantId":"K08 DK110335","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R35 GM142873","orderIn":0},{"agency":"U.S. Department of Health &amp; Human Services | National Institutes of Health","grantId":"K08-DK110335","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bacteria","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Clostridium","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adenosine Triphosphate","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Fermentation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Metabolomics"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Adenosine Triphosphate","registryNumber":"8L70Q75FXE"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9089323"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41564-022-01109-9"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9089323"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC9089323?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"35505246","reference":"Nat Microbiol. 2022 May;7(5):603-604. doi: 10.1038/s41564-022-01115-x","type":"Comment in","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":93,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS1792676","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2022-05-05","dateOfCreation":"2022-05-03","dateOfRevision":"2024-04-03","electronicPublicationDate":"2022-05-02","firstPublicationDate":"2022-05-02","firstIndexDate":"2022-05-04","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-05","embargoDate":"2022-11-02","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"23772070","source":"MED","pmid":"23772070","pmcid":"PMC3754583","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC3754583"]},"doi":"10.1128/jb.00321-13","title":"Effect of an oxygen-tolerant bifurcating butyryl coenzyme A dehydrogenase/electron-transferring flavoprotein complex from Clostridium difficile on butyrate production in Escherichia coli.","authorString":"Aboulnaga el-H, Pinkenburg O, Schiffels J, El-Refai A, Buckel W, Selmer T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Aboulnaga el-H","lastName":"Aboulnaga","firstName":"El-Hussiny","initials":"el-H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Aachen University of Applied Sciences, Campus Juelich, Department of Chemistry and Biotechnology, Juelich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Pinkenburg O","lastName":"Pinkenburg","firstName":"Olaf","initials":"O"},{"fullName":"Schiffels J","lastName":"Schiffels","firstName":"Johannes","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9674-9339"}},{"fullName":"El-Refai A","lastName":"El-Refai","firstName":"Ahmed","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Buckel W","lastName":"Buckel","firstName":"Wolfgang","initials":"W"},{"fullName":"Selmer T","lastName":"Selmer","firstName":"Thorsten","initials":"T"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9674-9339"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"16","volume":"195","journalIssueId":2059952,"dateOfPublication":"2013 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2013,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Bacteriol","title":"Journal of bacteriology","essn":"1098-5530","issn":"0021-9193","isoabbreviation":"J Bacteriol","nlmid":"2985120R"},"printPublicationDate":"2013-08-01"},"pubYear":"2013","pageInfo":"3704-3713","abstractText":"The butyrogenic genes from Clostridium difficile DSM 1296(T) have been cloned and expressed in Escherichia coli. The enzymes acetyl-coenzyme A (CoA) C-acetyltransferase, 3-hydroxybutyryl-CoA dehydrogenase, crotonase, phosphate butyryltransferase, and butyrate kinase and the butyryl-CoA dehydrogenase complex composed of the dehydrogenase and two electron-transferring flavoprotein subunits were individually produced in E. coli and kinetically characterized in vitro. While most of these enzymes were measured using well-established test systems, novel methods to determine butyrate kinase and butyryl-CoA dehydrogenase activities with respect to physiological function were developed. Subsequently, the individual genes were combined to form a single plasmid-encoded operon in a plasmid vector, which was successfully used to confer butyrate-forming capability to the host. In vitro and in vivo studies demonstrated that C. difficile possesses a bifurcating butyryl-CoA dehydrogenase which catalyzes the NADH-dependent reduction of ferredoxin coupled to the reduction of crotonyl-CoA also by NADH. Since the reoxidation of ferredoxin by a membrane-bound ferredoxin:NAD(+)-oxidoreductase enables electron transport phosphorylation, additional ATP is formed. The butyryl-CoA dehydrogenase from C. difficile is oxygen stable and apparently uses oxygen as a co-oxidant of NADH in the presence of air. These properties suggest that this enzyme complex might be well suited to provide butyryl-CoA for solventogenesis in recombinant strains. The central role of bifurcating butyryl-CoA dehydrogenases and membrane-bound ferredoxin:NAD oxidoreductases (Rhodobacter nitrogen fixation [RNF]), which affect the energy yield of butyrate fermentation in the clostridial metabolism, is discussed.","affiliation":"Aachen University of Applied Sciences, Campus Juelich, Department of Chemistry and Biotechnology, Juelich, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Escherichia coli","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Oxygen"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Butyrates","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Electron-Transferring Flavoproteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Butyryl-CoA Dehydrogenase","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bacterial Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Recombinant Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cloning, Molecular"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation, Bacterial","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","